<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.2.2: Constant and Exponential Change</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01758</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.2.2: Constant and Exponential Change</md:title>
    <md:uuid>618817ff-e0ac-4023-8e4b-641bec86f60d</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <para id="para-00001">Pattern 1</para>
      <para id="para-00002">
          <media alt="Pattern. Step 0,1 dot. Step 1, 3 dots, 2 by 2 with upper left dot removed. Step 2, 5 dots, 3 by 2 with upper left dot removed. Step 3, 7 dots, 4 by 2 with upper left dot removed.">
            <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_04_03_01.svg"/>
          </media>
        
     </para>
      <para id="para-00003">Pattern 2</para>
      <para id="para-00004">
          <media alt="Pattern. Step 0, 0 dots. Step 1, 1 dot. Step 2, 4 dots, arranged 2 by 2. Step 3, 9 dots, arranged 3 by 3.">
            <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_04_03_02.svg"/>
          </media>
        
     </para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00005">Look at Pattern 1.</para>
          <para id="para-00006">How is the number of dots in each step changing?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00007">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00008">Each step has 2 rows of dots, with each row having the same number of dots as the step number, plus one extra dot.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00009">How many dots will be in Step 5 in the pattern, and how will they be arranged?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00010">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00011">In Step 5, there will be 2 rows of 5 dots plus one additional dot.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00012">Look at Pattern 2.</para>
          <para id="para-00013">How is the number of dots in each step changing?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00014">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00015">Each step has as many rows and columns of dots as the step number.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00016">How many dots will be in Step 5 in the pattern, and how will they be arranged?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00017">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00018">Step 5 is a 5-by-5 array of dots.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <para id="para-00019">Consider the following table.</para>
      <table class="os-raise-wideadjustedtable" id="table-00001">
        <tgroup cols="3">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <entry>
                <emphasis> Step </emphasis>
              </entry>
              <entry>
                <emphasis> Number of dots in Pattern 1 </emphasis>
              </entry>
              <entry>
                <emphasis> Number of dots in Pattern 2 </emphasis>
              </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry> 0 </entry>
              <entry> 1 </entry>
              <entry> 0 </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry> 1 </entry>
              <entry> 3 </entry>
              <entry> 1 </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry> 2 </entry>
              <entry> 5 </entry>
              <entry> 4 </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry> 3 </entry>
              <entry> 7 </entry>
              <entry> 9 </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry> 4 </entry>
              <entry/>
              <entry/>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry> 5 </entry>
              <entry/>
              <entry/>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry> 10 </entry>
              <entry/>
              <entry/>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry> 12 </entry>
              <entry/>
              <entry/>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry> \(n\) </entry>
              <entry/>
              <entry/>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </tgroup>
      </table>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00001">
        <item>Complete the table with the number of dots for Pattern 1.</item>
      </list>
      <exercise id="_8095bb6a-6ede-482f-978e-394579c2a588" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00020">In Step 4, what is the number of dots in Pattern 1?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00021">Correct! Step 4 has 9 dots in Pattern 1.</para>
          <para id="para-00022">The correct answer is 9. Step 4 has 9 dots in Pattern 1.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_8139dd15-22b1-4be7-9a65-6a4492b88b72" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00023">In Step 5, what is the number of dots in Pattern 1?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00024">Correct! Step 5 has 11 dots in Pattern 1.</para>
          <para id="para-00025">The correct answer is 11. Step 5 has 11 dots in Pattern 1.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_9330201a-1aa8-48b6-b4c5-f7beef96cfe7" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00026">In Step 10, what is the number of dots in Pattern 1?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00027">Correct! Step 10 has 21 dots in Pattern 1.</para>
          <para id="para-00028">The correct answer is 21. Step 10 has 21 dots in Pattern 1.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="a74ee2de-72ef-4d03-bd65-da607a8f852e" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00008">
          <para id="para-00029">In Step 12, what is the number of dots in Pattern 1?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00008">
          <para id="para-00030">Correct! Step 12 has 25 dots in Pattern 1.</para>
          <para id="para-00031">The correct answer is 25. Step 12 has 25 dots in Pattern 1.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00009">
          <para id="para-00032">In Step \(n\), what is the number of dots in Pattern 1?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00009">
          <para id="para-00033">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00034">\(2n+1\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00002">
        <item>Complete the table with the number of dots for Pattern 2.</item>
      </list>
      <exercise id="_2b787d9d-44ad-4bb3-a433-1acdd39ddf49" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00010">
          <para id="para-00035">In Step 4, what is the number of dots in Pattern 2?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00010">
          <para id="para-00036">Correct! Step 4 has 16 dots in Pattern 2.</para>
          <para id="para-00037">The correct answer is 16. Step 4 has 16 dots in Pattern 2.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_277c86eb-6bf2-4c19-824e-49ea954daf66" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00011">
          <para id="para-00038">In Step 5, what is the number of dots in Pattern 2?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00011">
          <para id="para-00039">Correct! Step 5 has 25 dots in Pattern 2.</para>
          <para id="para-00040">The correct answer is 25. Step 5 has 25 dots in Pattern 2.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_95eaf6ac-0fbb-4732-9a7e-d258e1c80258" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00012">
          <para id="para-00041">In Step 10, what is the number of dots in Pattern 2?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00012">
          <para id="para-00042">Correct! Step 10 has 100 dots in Pattern 2.</para>
          <para id="para-00043">The correct answer is 100. Step 10 has 100 dots in Pattern 2.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="af51617b-17bb-4e0d-9cd3-c26d38266205" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00013">
          <para id="para-00044">In Step 12, what is the number of dots in Pattern 2?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00013">
          <para id="para-00045">Correct! Step 12 has 144 dots in Pattern 2.</para>
          <para id="para-00046">The correct answer is 144. Step 12 has 144 dots in Pattern 2.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00006">
        <problem id="prob-00014">
          <para id="para-00047">In Step \(n\), what is the number of dots in Pattern 2?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00014">
          <para id="para-00048">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00049">\(n \cdot n\) or \(n^2\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <media alt="...">
        <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/>
      </media>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00003">
        <item>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Plot the number of dots at each step number for Pattern 1 and Pattern 2.</item>
      </list>
      <exercise id="exer-00007">
        <problem id="prob-00015">
          <para id="para-00050">Pattern 1</para>
          <para id="para-00051">Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00015">
          <para id="para-00052">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00053">
                  <media alt="Points plotted on a grid. Number of dots. Step number.">
                <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_04_03_03.svg"/>
              </media>
            
         </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00008">
        <problem id="prob-00016">
          <para id="para-00054">Pattern 2</para>
          <para id="para-00055">Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00016">
          <para id="para-00056">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00057">
                  <media alt="Points plotted on a grid. Number of dots. Step number.">
                <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_04_03_04.svg"/>
              </media>
            
         </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00009">
        <problem id="prob-00017">
          <para id="para-00058">Explain why the graphs of the two patterns look the way they do.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00017">
          <para id="para-00059">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00060">The points representing the first pattern lie on a line because getting from one step to the next involves adding the same number of dots (2 dots). The points representing the second pattern do not lie on a line. They “curve” upward because the number of dots added at each step increases from step to step.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <para id="para-00061">
          <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_04_04_01.svg"/>
          </media>
        
     </para>
      <para id="para-00062">How many squares will be in Step 5?</para>
    </note>
    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      <section id="sect-00003">
        <title>Writing Expressions for Patterns</title>
        <para><emphasis id="para-00063">Example 1</emphasis></para>
        <para id="para-00064">
              <media alt="DIAGRAM WITH 3 FIGURES. STEP 1 IS SHAPED LIKE A PLUS SIGN, WITH 1 SQUARE IN THE MIDDLE AND 1 SQUARE ATTACHED TO EACH OF ITS SIDES. STEP 2 HAS A RECTANGLE WITH 2 ROWS AND 4 COLUMNS. ABOVE AND BELOW THE SECOND COLUMN OF THE RECTANGLE IS 1 SQUARE. STEP 3 HAS A RECTANGLE WITH 3 ROWS AND 5 COLUMNS. ABOVE AND BELOW THE SECOND COLUMN OF THE RECTANGLE IS 1 SQUARE.
">
              <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_04_05_01.jpg"/>
            </media>
          
       </para>
        <para id="para-00065">Find the number of squares in Step 5:</para>
        <para id="para-00066">First, look to see the pattern. Every step, another row and another column are added, plus the two extra squares.</para>
        <list id="list-00004">
          <item>Step 1: 1 row of \(3 + 2\)</item>
          <item>Step 2: 2 rows of \(4 +2\)</item>
          <item>Step 3: 3 rows of \(5 + 2\)</item>
        </list>
        <para id="para-00067">So the width is always 1 by \(n\), or \(n\). The length is \(n+2\), and then another 2 is added.</para>
        <para id="para-00068">Then, draw pictures or create a table:</para>
        <table class="os-raise-midsizetable" id="table-00002">
          <tgroup cols="2">
            <tbody>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis> Step </emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis> # of squares </emphasis>
                </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  <para id="para-00069">1</para>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <para id="para-00070">5</para>
                </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  <para id="para-00071">2</para>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <para id="para-00072">10</para>
                </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  <para id="para-00073">3</para>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <para id="para-00074">17</para>
                </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  <para id="para-00075">4</para>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <para id="para-00076">26</para>
                </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  <para id="para-00077">5</para>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <para id="para-00078">37</para>
                </entry>
              </row>
            </tbody>
          </tgroup>
        </table>
        <para id="para-00079">At Step 5, there are 37 squares.</para>
        <para><emphasis id="para-00080">Example 2</emphasis></para>
        <para id="para-00081">Find the expression for Step \(n\).</para>
        <para id="para-00082">Multiply the length and width, and then add the 2 extra squares:</para>
        <para id="para-00083">\(n(n+2)+2 = n^2+2n+n\)</para>
        <note class="try" id="note-00002">
          <title>Try It: Writing Expressions for Patterns</title>
      <exercise id="exer-00010">
            <problem id="prob-00018">
              <para id="para-00084">Use the figure below to find the number of squares in Step 5 and then Step \(n\).</para>
              <para id="para-00085">
                          <media alt="DIAGRAM WITH 3 FIGURES. STEP 1 HAS A RECTANGLE WITH 2 ROWS AND 3 COLUMNS. 2 SQUARES OF A THIRD ROW ARE ADDED. STEP 2 HAS A RECTANGLE WITH 3 ROWS AND 4 COLUMNS. 3 SQUARES OF A FOURTH ROW ARE ADDED. STEP 3 HAS A RECTANGLE WITH 4 ROWS AND 5 COLUMNS. 4 SQUARES OF A FIFTH ROW ARE ADDED.
">
                    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_04_05_02.jpg"/>
                  </media>
                
             </para>
              <para id="para-00086">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00018">
              <para id="para-00087">Here is how to determine the number of squares in each step:</para>
              <para id="para-00088">Notice that each figure adds a row and column, and then subtracts one square. The number of rows is always 2 more than the step number.</para>
              <para id="para-00089">Begin by making a table:</para>
              <table class="os-raise-midsizetable" id="table-00003">
                <tgroup cols="2">
                  <tbody>
                    <row>
                      <entry>
                        <emphasis> Step </emphasis>
                      </entry>
                      <entry>
                        <emphasis> # of squares </emphasis>
                      </entry>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                      <entry> 1 </entry>
                      <entry> 8 </entry>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                      <entry> 2 </entry>
                      <entry> 15 </entry>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                      <entry> 3 </entry>
                      <entry> 24 </entry>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                      <entry> 4 </entry>
                      <entry> 35 </entry>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                      <entry> 5 </entry>
                      <entry> 48 </entry>
                    </row>
                  </tbody>
                </tgroup>
              </table>
              <para id="para-00090">There are 48 squares in Step 5.</para>
              <para id="para-00091">Step \(n\) would be found with \((n+2)(n+2)-1\) or \((n+2)^2-1\).</para>
            </solution>
          </exercise>
        </note>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>